      <AdSlot slotType="leaderboard" id="qualifiers-mid-ad" />

      <!-- Section 3: Breakthroughs -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Regional Breakthroughs &amp; Historic Firsts</h2>
        
        <h3>🌍 Africa's Historic Two-Spot Era</h3>
        <p>
          Following landmark performances by African competitors at Alexandra Palace, the PDC and the <strong>African Darts Group (ADG)</strong> have expanded the continent's representation to <strong>two guaranteed berths</strong> for the first time. The dedicated regional split ensures permanent geographic representation: Kenya's <strong>David Munyua</strong> secured the Northern Africa spot in Nairobi, while the Southern Africa qualifier takes place in Cape Town.
        </p>

        <h3>🇲🇴 Macau's Historic Ally Pally Debut</h3>
        <p>
          The 2026/27 World Championship marks the tournament debut of Macau. <strong>Kam Weng Cheng</strong> made history by storming into the final of the PDC Asian Championship, earning his place on darts' grandest stage and becoming the first darts player from Macau ever to compete at Alexandra Palace.
        </p>

        <h3>🎯 Women's Professional Representation</h3>
        <p>
          Women's professional darts continues its rapid ascendancy with five female competitors confirmed for Alexandra Palace. Led by reigning Women's World Matchplay champion <a href="/players/beau-greaves/">Beau Greaves</a>, the contingent features global icon <a href="/players/fallon-sherrock/">Fallon Sherrock</a>, four-time World Champion Lisa Ashton, multi-title winner Gemma Hayter, and Sweden's Vicky Pruim. Read our comprehensive analysis of the <a href="/tournament/pdc-womens-series/">PDC Women's Series</a> for seasonal tour details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Road Ahead -->
